# QuickBooks Online MCP — AI Orchestration Rules

You have access to the `quickbooks` MCP server, a **read-only** bridge to the
operator's QuickBooks Online company file. These rules cover how to use it well.

Read `AGENTS.md` in this workspace for the behavioural brief. This file is the
tool-level reference.

## Hard constraint: this connection cannot write

The server runs with `QUICKBOOKS_DISABLE_WRITE`, `QUICKBOOKS_DISABLE_UPDATE`
and `QUICKBOOKS_DISABLE_DELETE` all `true`. The 71 `create_*` / `update_*` /
`delete_*` tools are never registered. Only 71 read tools exist.

When asked to change something, describe the change precisely and hand it to the
operator to do in the QuickBooks UI. Do not look for a workaround, and do not
suggest re-enabling writes as a casual next step — that is a production
systemd change.

## Session opener

| Tool | When | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `get_company_info` | First call of every session | Confirms the connection works and that you're pointed at the expected company file |
| `get_preferences` | Before any analysis | Reveals accounting method (cash vs accrual) and fiscal year start — both change every number you report |

If `get_company_info` fails, do not proceed to guess at data. Report the error
verbatim and stop; see `AGENTS.md` → Escalation.

## Reports (prefer these over reconstruction)

| Tool | Question it answers |
|---|---|
| `get_profit_and_loss` | Revenue, expenses, net income for a period |
| `get_balance_sheet` | Assets, liabilities, equity at a date |
| `get_cash_flow` | Operating / investing / financing cash movement |
| `get_trial_balance` | All account balances — the reconciliation starting point |
| `get_general_ledger` | Transaction-level detail per account |
| `get_aged_receivables` | Who owes money, bucketed by age |
| `get_aged_payables` | What is owed to vendors, bucketed by age |
| `get_customer_balance` / `get_customer_sales` | Per-customer position and revenue |
| `get_vendor_balance` / `get_vendor_expenses` | Per-vendor position and spend |

**Never hand-compute a figure a report already produces.** Summing invoices to
derive revenue ignores journal entries, credit memos, accrual timing and
adjustments, so your total will disagree with what the operator sees on screen —
and their screen wins. Use `search_*` to *explain* a report number, not to
replace it.

## Search tools

`search_accounts`, `search_attachables`, `search_bill_payments`, `search_bills`,
`search_budgets`, `search_classes`, `search_credit_memos`, `search_customers`,
`search_departments`, `search_deposits`, `search_employees`, `search_estimates`,
`search_invoices`, `search_items`, `search_journal_entries`,
`search_payment_methods`, `search_payments`, `search_purchase_orders`,
`search_purchases`, `search_refund_receipts`, `search_sales_receipts`,
`search_tax_agencies`, `search_tax_codes`, `search_tax_rates`, `search_terms`,
`search_time_activities`, `search_transfers`, `search_vendor_credits`,
`search_vendors`.

Always pass an explicit date range. Results are paginated — an unbounded search
on a mature company file returns a truncated window that looks complete but
isn't. If a result set hits the page limit, say so rather than treating it as
the full picture.

## Single-record fetches

`get_account`, `get_attachable`, `get_bill_payment`, `get_class`,
`get_credit_memo`, `get_customer`, `get_department`, `get_deposit`,
`get_employee`, `get_estimate`, `get_invoice_pdf`, `get_journal_entry`,
`get_payment`, `get_payment_method`, `get_purchase`, `get_purchase_order`,
`get_refund_receipt`, `get_sales_receipt`, `get_tax_agency`, `get_tax_code`,
`get_tax_rate`, `get_term`, `get_time_activity`, `get_transfer`,
`get_vendor_credit`, `get-bill`, `get-vendor`, `read_invoice`, `read_item`.

Note the two hyphenated legacy names (`get-bill`, `get-vendor`) — they are
spelled that way upstream.

## Orchestration patterns

**"How did we do last quarter?"**
`get_preferences` → `get_profit_and_loss` for the period → if anything looks
off, `get_general_ledger` on the specific account → `search_*` for the
individual transactions.

**"Who owes us money?"**
`get_aged_receivables` → `search_invoices` filtered to unpaid for the specific
customers → `get_customer` for contact context.

**"Why is this expense category so high?"**
`get_profit_and_loss` to confirm the delta is real → `get_general_ledger` for
that account → `search_purchases` / `search_bills` over the period →
`get_vendor_expenses` to see if it concentrates in one vendor.

**"Are the books clean?"**
`get_trial_balance` → look for an Uncategorised Income / Uncategorised Expense /
Ask My Accountant balance → `search_journal_entries` and `search_purchases` to
find what's sitting there. Report findings; do not reclassify (you can't).

## Reporting standards

- Quote every figure with its basis and source: *"$X (accrual, per
  `get_profit_and_loss`, 2026-04-01..2026-06-30)"*.
- State the accounting method whenever you report a period figure. Cash vs
  accrual can differ by a lot and the operator may not specify which they meant.
- Flag data-quality caveats up front: unreconciled accounts, uncategorised
  balances, an open period. A confident number from unreconciled books is worse
  than a caveated one.
- Summarise first, offer detail second. Do not paste raw JSON.
- No tax or legal conclusions. Report what the books say and point at the CPA.
